<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qusayr Amra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qusayr Amra is a small Umayyad desert palace and bath-house in eastern Jordan, built in the early eighth century, probably for the prince al-Walid ibn Yazid, later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Walid II, between about 723 and 743. Its walls carry the largest surviving cycle of early Islamic figurative painting, with hunting and bathing scenes, images of foreign rulers and a dome painted with the zodiac. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qusayr-amra.jpg" alt="Qusayr Amra" width="320" height="197" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Case · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qusayr_Amra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Qusayr 'Amra, also written Quseir Amra or Qasr Amra, is the best known of the desert castles of eastern Jordan. It stands on the north side of Highway 40, about 85 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> and 21 kilometres south-west of Al-Azraq. What survives is the remnant of a larger complex of about 25 hectares that included a castle meant as a royal retreat, without military function, of which only the foundation remains. Its placement was governed by access to the ancient routes running north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, including the one from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayma">Tayma</a> through Wadi Sirhan to the plain of Balqa.</p><p>A painting of six kings that includes the Visigothic king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a>, who reigned 710 to 712, led researchers for a long time to date the building to about 710 and to the caliph Walid I. An inscription found in 2012 dated it to the two decades from 723 to 743, when it was commissioned by Walid ibn Yazid, crown prince under the caliph Hisham and caliph himself in 743 and 744. Walid and Yazid, two princes who each later became caliph, spent long periods away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, the Umayyad capital, before taking the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The surviving building is a small hall and bathhouse of limestone and basalt, made of rough masonry bound with gypsum-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lime-mortar">lime mortar</a> in a Sasanian tradition. The bathhouse is one of the oldest surviving remains of a hammam in the historic Muslim world. Its frescoes, mostly on the ceilings, show hunting, bathing and dancing scenes, working craftsmen, animals and plants, and a cycle of Jonah, the earliest known images of Jonah in Islamic art. The six kings are identified as the Byzantine emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roderic">Roderic</a>, the Sasanian shah, the Negus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and the emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and their meaning is unclear, though a Greek word for victory found nearby suggests the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s supremacy over his enemies.</p><p>The hemispherical dome of the hot room shows the zodiac and 35 identifiable constellations, believed to be the earliest image of the night sky painted on anything other than a flat surface. Its only discernible error, the counterclockwise order of the stars, suggests it was copied from a flat original.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Alois Musil rediscovered the abandoned building in 1898, and drawings of the frescoes by Alphons Leopold Mielich in his book made them known. A Spanish team restored the frescoes in the 1970s, and the shellac applied then was removed in 1996. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> inscribed the site as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1985. A conservation project begun in 2010 by the World Monuments Fund, an Italian institute and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Antiquities of Jordan also covers the exterior buildings, and cleaning has revealed rich colours.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qusayr 'Amra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The court at Damascus, together with the desert palaces such as <b>Qusayr Amra</b>, adopted elements of Byzantine and Persian royal display, which pious critics condemned as incompatible with the egalitarian ideals of early Islam.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
